# happy-dsh — Caddy with a real certificate issued via DNS-01, no inbound ports.
#
# Use this when the host is NOT reachable from the public internet but you still
# want a publicly trusted certificate. The DNS-01 challenge proves control of
# the domain by writing a TXT record, so ACME never needs to reach the host.
#
# The result: a real certificate for a name that resolves only on your own
# network. This is what makes a *private* deployment work in a browser without
# installing a private CA on every client (which on iOS means a manual profile
# install plus toggling full trust — doable, but this avoids it entirely).
#
# ⚠️  A publicly trusted certificate means the hostname is published to
# Certificate Transparency logs. Do not treat the name as a secret.
#
# ── Build ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Stock Caddy does NOT ship the Cloudflare DNS provider. Build one that does:
#
#     xcaddy build --with github.com/caddy-dns/cloudflare
#
# ── Credentials ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A Cloudflare API token with `Zone → DNS → Edit` on the zone, exported as
# CF_API_TOKEN. Scope it to the single zone. Do not use a Global API Key.
#
#     CF_API_TOKEN=... caddy run --config Caddyfile.dns01-cloudflare
#
# ── Pair with a local DNS entry ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Create NO public A/AAAA record. Point the name at the host in whatever serves
# DNS for your network, then start DSH with the same authority:
#
#     dsh web --no-open --trusted-host dsh.example.com

{
	email you@example.com
}

dsh.example.com {
	tls {
		dns cloudflare {env.CF_API_TOKEN}
		resolvers 1.1.1.1
	}

	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3080

	# The browser-session cookie is currently issued WITHOUT `Secure`, even over
	# TLS, because DSH's shipped transport assumes loopback HTTP. Recording it
	# here so the gap is visible rather than surprising:
	# https://github.com/fkysly/happy-dsh — see README "Cookie attributes".
	#
	# Caddy supplies HSTS and the modern TLS defaults on its own. Everything the
	# browser sends to this authority is encrypted, so the cookie is protected
	# in transit; the missing attribute only means a browser would also be
	# willing to send it over plaintext to the same authority, which you are not
	# serving.
}
